cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or manuscript, please cite it as below."
title: "Delayed Collapse of a Degenerate Core at Its Maximum Mass: General-Relativistic Instability and the Fold-Passage Law"
abstract: >-
  Paper B of this series finds that no known classical field theory sources the mass-dependent regularized core
  studied in Papers A and B. This paper asks the dynamical question that leaves open: what does a degenerate
  stellar core do as it is pushed to its maximum mass, and how long does it take? For the idealized cold
  electron gas we (i) locate the maximum mass with the Tolman-Oppenheimer-Volkoff equation (M_max = 1.4166
  M_sun, within 0.07% of the Chandrasekhar-Tooper value); (ii) implement Chandrasekhar's general-relativistic
  pulsation equation and tighten the fundamental eigenvalue by Rayleigh-Ritz to sigma^2 = -28.55 s^-2 (e-folding
  time 0.187 s) at the test configuration, passing the exact turning-point test (the eigenvalue vanishes where
  the mass peaks) to 0.01% for five equations of state, a test our first implementation failed and that exposed
  two errors we correct and document; (iii) take the nonlinear outcome (collapse, not migration, for compressive
  perturbations) from the established numerical-relativity literature; (iv) build a calibrated one-degree-of-
  freedom nonlinear model, compared with an independent spherical general-relativistic Lagrangian evolution,
  which reproduces the time to half the initial radius to 0.4% (a test mainly of the shared fold coefficients)
  and the plunge to 1-3%; and (v) treat a star that reaches the maximum by slow accretion as a ramped saddle-
  node bifurcation described by the first Painleve equation, which gives a collapse delay t = 3.41 (4a/(kappa^2
  Mdot))^(1/5) in terms of two exact-GR fold coefficients, a seed set by the ramp, a mass overshoot below 1e-10
  M_sun (conservative case; at most 1e-8 M_sun with damping), a 1-2% composition dependence (12% for a
  counterfactual 56Fe star), and a location unaffected by dissipation, which only lengthens the delay. The use
  of Painleve I at a fold of the equilibrium sequence of a self-gravitating fluid is not new (Newtonian
  supernova cores: Pomeau et al., Chavanis et al.); the application at the general-relativistic turning point
  with exact fold coefficients, and the delay law, are new as far as we found. The Lagrangian evolution reaches
  a first trapped shell of 0.25 M_sun, but that, like every timescale here, is a property of the idealized gas,
  which neither neutronizes nor bounces; Paper D replaces it by a dense-matter equation of state and finds a
  bounce.
